# --- Secrets -----------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY: a committed .env is the single most common way production credentials
# leak. Git history is permanent — deleting the file later does NOT remove the
# secret from earlier commits, and anyone who ever cloned the repo keeps it.
# Only .env.example (placeholders, no real values) belongs in version control.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets.json

# Audit logs contain real personal and payroll data.
audit.log

# Locally held mail outbox. Never in version control: it holds verification,
# recovery and invitation codes in plaintext. Git history is permanent, so a
# single committed file is a permanent disclosure. (KYC documents now live in
# S3-compatible object storage, not on local disk — see src/storage/kyc.ts.)
.data/outbox/
